  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential mechanism by which Santolina triene may interact with SMMC-7721, a cancer cell line. The drug–disease link is supported through multiple pathways, with Santolina triene acting as a target for ETK-1, a protein that is also a target of hesperetin and another compound, (4R)-3-methylidene-4-prop-1-en-2-ylcyclohexene. These compounds all ultimately target SMMC-7721, indicating a shared biological pathway. The evidence is consistent with a mechanism involving the inhibition of a target protein, which may lead to the suppression of cancer cell growth. The presence of multiple targets and their shared downstream effect on SMMC-7721 suggests a possible role in cancer progression or treatment resistance. The integration of these pathways supports the hypothesis that Santolina triene may exert its effects through a mechanism involving the modulation of ETK-1, which could be relevant in the context of cancer therapy. The evidence is robust and aligns with the expected biological roles of these compounds, though further experimental validation would be necessary to confirm the exact mechanism and therapeutic potential.",
